White gold at this price point delivers the full east-west aesthetic with added setting sophistication. The shank design gives the band more presence alongside the horizontal stone. For step-cut diamonds where table clarity is everything, white gold provides the clearest visual backdrop.

**East West Diamond Engagement Ring in 14k Yellow Gold — [$1,635](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/east-west-diamond-engagement-ring-in-14k-yellow-gold-item-193951?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)** (4 reviews)

Yellow gold at this tier pairs the warm metal with a more detailed setting that complements the horizontal stone placement. The design detailing on the shank adds visual interest beyond what the simple solitaire provides without adding stone weight.

**East West Diamond Engagement Ring in 14k Rose Gold — [$1,635](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/east-west-diamond-engagement-ring-in-14k-rose-gold-item-193777?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)** (4 reviews)

Rose gold here creates the most contemporary east-west look in the mid-range tier. The $1,635 price is identical across YG, WG, and RG — choose purely on aesthetic preference.

**East West Diamond Engagement Ring in Platinum — [$2,600](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/east-west-diamond-engagement-ring-in-platinum-item-193952?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)** (4 reviews)

The most expensive non-pavé east-west setting. Platinum adds $965 over 14k gold at this tier. The durability advantage is identical to the solitaire platinum argument — east-west prong placement takes more lateral wear, and platinum handles it better.

